Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.030 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to welcome today John tie to  the program a former NSA whistleblower  and a lawyer who back in 2017 founded  whistleblower aid which has provided  support to the anonymous Ukraine  whistleblower among many other things  John great to have you on today thank  you so much David so let's start with  your path executive order 12333 what is  it and how did that turn you into a  whistleblower thanks I was at the State  Department I worked in the Human Rights  Bureau from 2011 to 2014 on Internet  freedom and in that role I had a top  secret clearance so I was reading  intelligence from various countries  around the world and in June of 2013 the  Snowden disclosures became very public  news and I was part of the diplomatic  response to those disclosures both with  Germany Brazil other countries the  United Nations the Human Rights Council  in Geneva and during that period in the  in the nine months following that I got  a couple of briefings from the NSA on  the scope of u.s. signals intelligence  collection and what I learned was for  that whole year following the Snowden  leaks there hadn't been a single story  you despite front-page stories almost  every day hadn't been a single story on  the main legal authority that the NSA is  using to collect Americans data which is  as you said executive order 12 triple  three I was concerned about this I was  interested obviously care very deeply  about human rights and Americans privacy  and so basically this is a legal  loophole where the NSA is using an  authority originally intended to collect  foreigners data to actually sweep up  most of what we do online including US  persons American citizens this concerned  me  it wasn't being addressed in the in the  policy responses to the Snowden  disclosures and during that period I  became a whistleblower I did not want to  go to prison  I did not want to leave the country and  so I hired a couple of lawyers I paid  them thirteen thousand dollars this  would have been in 2014  probably March April 2014 and went  through the lawful disclosure process so  I met with the State Department  Inspector General the NSA Inspector  General House and Senate intelligence  committees and others and eventually  went through the pre-publication review  process and published an op-ed in The  Washington Post about this problem  so essentially you believe that order 12  triple three was being misused in order  to surveil Americans when as as it was  written you believe it was not meant to  apply to Americans yeah that's right it  was originally before the Internet there  were two legal regimes a statutory  regime for intelligence agency  operations inside the United States  which is under FISA the Foreign  Intelligence Surveillance Act and then  twelve triple three was meant to cover  all the operations outside of our  borders with the internet that  distinction between inside our borders  and outside our borders became almost  irrelevant and a whole bunch of  Americans data is stored in data centers  around the world and it's treated as  foreign persons the data and collected  as such by the NSA when we think about  the government's treatment of prominent  and again even the use of the term  whistleblower versus leaker it's the  subject of debate and controversy so I'm  not trying to sort of like Prime the  discussion in any particular way but  when we think about the treatment of  folks like Edward Snowden Chelsea  Manning etc you have a very wide range  of opinion as to how these individuals  should be treated on one side you have  the idea that strictly speaking if  information was released in violation of  of how that information is classified or  if the information was obtained in a way  that violates the law or whatever that  that's essentially all that matters that  the content really does in an individual  should be punished as such on the other  side you have those who believe that  these are merely heroic acts that should  be celebrated and acted upon to change  what is the misbehavior of the  government including as you say you know  the misuse of an executive order  executive order in the case that you  discuss what is your general approaches  to how individuals should be treated by  the government and by the justice system  well our motto at whistleblower aid is  report government and corporate law  breaking without breaking the law so we  are a legal organization we would not  never help anyone to leak classified  information with that said these are  very difficult questions there aren't  easy answers and I think reasonable  people can disagree on some of this  stuff you know I we created this  organization so that people wouldn't  have to choose between you know  following their conscience and you know  avoiding harm to themselves so so we try  to help people follow the available  lawful disclosure process there are  often more options available than many  people realize and you know open up more  options so that people can keep their  careers don't have to go to prison and  still try to try to inform the public  about about what's going wrong you know  with those cases you mentioned those you  know those people didn't feel like they  had other options and you know we are  here to try to to help people not have  to put their career or their freedom in  jeopardy not not to be can overly  conspiratorial or dystopian or whatever  but what one can imagine situations  where one would believe that the  information they are trying to disclose  or what they are trying to blow the  whistle on is so damaging to the  government that going through the  process you describe might put them in  jeopardy might put them in danger might  allow them maybe to be silenced so is it  at least theoretically conceivable that  people could be in a position where they  want to go the route that you help  people go  but they believe that starting that  process actually puts them at risk or  may prevent the information from going  public yeah absolutely it's a risk  there's no easy things here and the more  the more important the disclosure the  more significant it is the more risky it  is for the individual and there's no way  around that  we're doing everything we can to  minimize those risks but the very first  thing I tell people when they come to me  is look the safest thing you can do is  go home and never talk to anyone about  this again so you know most people by  the time they get to me they've they've  crossed that bridge and they're there  they want to explore their options for  going forward but you know it's it's  risky and I would never say otherwise  can you talk a little bit for folks in  our audience who might be thinking about  WikiLeaks and the way that WikiLeaks has  been an intermediary with much  information that have been released can  you talk about how what you do is  completely different than what WikiLeaks  does absolutely we are not WikiLeaks we  do not help people leak classified  information we would never you know help  someone Julian Assange has been accused  of attempting to help someone to to hack  a government database we would never  help anyone try to do that you know look  that organisation has been credibly  accused of you know working wittingly or  unwittingly with Russian intelligence we  would never do anything like that I  swore an oath to the US Constitution  when I was at the State Department I'm  still down by that oath and take that  very seriously  so so we are very different from that  approach you know with that said I'll  say I don't agree with all of the  charges against the sons in the sense  that you know if the allegations are  true and I don't know the facts it  certainly would be illegal to try to  help someone hack a government database  I think publishing things received from  someone else shouldn't is not illegal  and should not be illegal so it's a  complicated legal matter when it comes  to the Ukraine  whistle blower can you talk a little bit  about do you believe that I mean this is  an individual who became the the target  of so many prominent people in the  public eye in the lead-up to Donald  Trump's eventual impeachment etc from  your perspective what what did they go  through this process in the correct way  absolutely so they immediately sought  legal counsel my colleague Andrew Bach I  and Mark Zaid were the lawyers for that  who are part of our team here at  whistleblower aid from the very  beginning they were planning how to do  this in a lawful way that would not  expose the client to criminal  prosecution or even dismissal from their  job inside a US intelligence agency that  person is still employed they did follow  all the rules they are entitled to legal  protections I think they're an example  of how the system of how people do  sometimes have more options than they  then they know about the legal process  here are very obscure some of these  statutes haven't been tested very often  and so we saw that with the attempts to  get this disclosure to Congress it was  bumpy the White House did try to block  Congress from receiving this in  violation of the laws but ultimately the  lawyers Android mark were able to get it  to Congress so I certainly wouldn't say  that our system is flawless but there  are often more options than people  understand and in this case the the  client did an amazing job in his true  hero were you able to or let me phrase  in a different way before the attempts  to out and create backlash against the  whistleblower started had you already  anticipated that it would reach the  degree that it did and how did you  advise the client to be prepared for  that in terms of security in terms of  practical things well first of all I  never had any direct contact with the  client Andrew and Mark Mike  colleagues were the ones representing  the client yeah you as they weren't  dressing yes yeah our firm so so I can't  confirm or deny any kind of you know  things about that person's identity or  anything like that broadly speaking as  soon as it was clear that this  implicated the president in the 2020  election yes it was obvious that this  was going to be a very high-profile  matter that they're going to be security  concerns from day one we brought in  top-level security people on the digital  side on the physical side as you may  know Mark Zaid one of the lawyers he was  getting death threats  we had armed guards on Mark Zaid 24  hours a day for months we and now the  Department of Justice is bringing a  criminal prosecution against one of the  individuals who was threatening to hurt  mark so it was a very serious situation  we knew that from the beginning and from  the beginning we were taking steps  that's really all I can say is it is  this once you understand the scope of  what is going on and again without  revealing anything particular in general  terms do you do extra I mean I know that  you say you always say to a lot of the  folks that come to you the easiest thing  for you is to go back home and never  speak about this again but are there  more conversations than normal of that  type given these particular  circumstances  III was not I I never had a conversation  with this client so I'm not gonna speak  to that fair enough  when we look going forward is there  anything that you believe took place in  or surrounding the Ukraine whistleblower  situation that may sort of change the  way these things are potentially handled  in the future both by a firm like yours  representing someone like this but also  the way that Congress the House of  Representatives and others worked in  receiving the information yeah clearly  threats intimidation starting  with the President of the United States  the president's family senior officials  US senators attempts to identify  intimidate this person are extremely  harmful arguably illegal in some cases  you you know you could make the argument  that any kind of presidential effort to  intimidate a whistleblower itself  constitutes you know an offense and so  there were serious problems here some of  those people should be held accountable  for their actions  it is never right to try to identify a  whistleblower with with the knowledge  that's you know some extremists would  would would threaten that person  possibly act violently against that  person um there need to be stronger  protections in place arguably that kind  of behavior should be criminalized which  it's not today except in certain narrow  circumstances and you know Congress  needs to identify the gaps that were  apparent in the system and and filled  them with legislation and certainly  there needs to be reforms Andrew and  Mark published New York Times op-ed two  or three weeks ago that laid out some of  their ideas for that so clearly there's  room for improvement yes certainly an  understatement I would say room for  improvement for for sure as in regards  to a lot of the elements of how this all  went down  John tie former NSA whistleblower and  attorney who in 2017 founded  whistleblower aide John so great to  speak to you and I really appreciate  your time today thanks David

